(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ophthalmology-i-vignettes"]={"title":"Common Ophthalmological Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a moderate bacterial corneal ulcer that sits directly in front of his pupil. How does its position affect the outlook?","choices":["The prognosis is better, because central ulcers are noticed and treated sooner","The prognosis depends only on the organism","The prognosis is worse, because ulcers inside the visual axis carry a poorer outcome","Position makes no difference; only ulcer size matters"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck grades prognosis by both size and position relative to the visual axis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 51-year-old outdoor worker has a yellowish raised nodule on the conjunctiva at the three o'clock position of one eye. It stops at the edge of the cornea and does not encroach on it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pinguecula","Pterygium","Xanthelasma","Episcleritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it sits at the classic position and spares the cornea. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on warfarin notices a bright red patch on the white of one eye after a coughing fit. There is no pain, vision is normal, the pupil is normal and the cornea is clear. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Anterior uveitis","Scleritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Valsalva plus anticoagulation, and the eye is otherwise entirely normal. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has splashed drain cleaner into one eye and arrives in distress. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Patch the eye and arrange same-day ophthalmology review afterwards","Begin copious irrigation immediately, before completing the history or examination, then check that the surface pH has normalised","Measure visual acuity in both eyes first, then irrigate","Obtain the full history first, then irrigate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chemical exposure is the single stated exception to the normal red-eye sequence. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man developed a tender red lump at his eyelid margin overnight. Two weeks of warm compresses have not helped and it persists. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue compresses for another three months","Start oral corticosteroids","Perform incision and drainage in the clinic yourself","Refer to ophthalmology for incision and drainage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a hordeolum that has not improved in two weeks meets the deck's referral threshold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man who grew up in a region with poor sanitation has inturned eyelashes rubbing his cornea, with scarring of the upper eyelid and a history of repeated childhood eye infections. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adult inclusion conjunctivitis","Blepharitis","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Trachoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — repeated chlamydial infection scars the lid, causing entropion and then trichiasis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a painful red eye with photophobia. Fluorescein under cobalt blue light shows a branching corneal lesion with small knobs at the ends of each branch (terminal end bulbs). Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral antiviral therapy for ten days","Topical corticosteroid drops started today","A topical fluoroquinolone for ten days","Oral doxycycline for seven days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a true dendrite with terminal end bulbs is pathognomonic for herpes simplex. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with ectropion asks what will actually fix it. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["It will resolve on its own within two to four weeks","A course of oral antibiotics will resolve it","Surgery is the definitive treatment; lubrication only protects the surface in the meantime","Lubricating ointment used consistently will gradually correct the lid position"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck names surgery as definitive. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 20-year-old woman has bilateral red, intensely itchy eyes with swollen lids and stringy watery discharge every April. Vision is normal, the corneas are clear, and there is no preauricular node. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute bacterial conjunctivitis","Chlamydial conjunctivitis","Acute allergic conjunctivitis","Acute viral conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — itch, bilaterality, seasonality and stringy discharge, with no node. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with untreated orbital cellulitis is deteriorating. Which complications does the lecture specifically warn about?","choices":["Intracranial spread causing meningitis or cavernous sinus thrombosis","Corneal perforation and endophthalmitis","Entropion progressing to trichiasis","Retinal detachment with vitreous haemorrhage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck's two named complications of untreated infection. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has been diagnosed with chlamydial conjunctivitis confirmed on conjunctival testing. Beyond treating her eye, which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the conjunctival test in one week to confirm that it has cleared","Refer her urgently to ophthalmology","Screen her for autoimmune disease","Arrange evaluation for other sexually transmitted infections and notify her partners for evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the eye finding is a sentinel for a usually asymptomatic urogenital infection. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman needs the front of her eye examined in magnified detail — the lids, cornea, conjunctiva, sclera and iris. Which instrument is designed for this?","choices":["A direct ophthalmoscope","A tonometer pen","A Wood lamp","A slit lamp"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a low-power microscope with a high-intensity slit beam for the anterior structures. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with a chronically red eye has pale bumps on the inner surface of the lower lid that look redder at their base. What do these indicate?","choices":["Drusen, which point to macular degeneration","Follicles, which point to a chlamydial or viral cause","Papillae, which point to a bacterial or allergic cause","Keratic precipitates, which point to anterior uveitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — follicles are pale at the surface and redder at the base. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has had a lid nodule in the same spot for four months, and it has recurred twice in the past year. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology to rule out sebaceous carcinoma","Continue warm compresses for another three months","Start oral antibiotics for two weeks","Reassure her that recurrence is normal and no action is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence, or persistence beyond two to three months, triggers this specific concern. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with rosacea has months of burning, gritty eyes with crusting and scaling at the lash bases and thickened, toothpaste-like secretion from the lid margins. Which is the most appropriate first-line management?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral","Lid hygiene","Topical antibiotic ointment","Oral antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck's first step, escalating only if two weeks of it fails. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a tender, tense, erythematous swelling over the nasal aspect of her lower eyelid, below the medial canthal tendon, with mucoid material expressible from the lower punctum. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dacryocystitis","A lacrimal sac tumour","Dacryoadenitis","Chalazion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the location below the tendon and the expressible discharge are both characteristic. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has two days of mild ache and a sectoral patch of redness in one eye, with no discharge and no photophobia. Which bedside test best confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Instil fluorescein and examine under cobalt blue light for a corneal dendrite","Measure the intraocular pressure with a tonometer pen","Obtain computed tomography of the orbits with contrast","Instil 2.5 per cent phenylephrine and look for blanching of the vessels after 15 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — episcleral vessels blanch; the mild painless sectoral redness fits episcleritis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has a lower eyelid that sags outward, with constant tearing and exposure of the inner lid surface. Which is the most appropriate initial management while awaiting definitive treatment?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid drops","Oral antibiotics for ten days","Preservative-free artificial tears during the day, lubricating ointment at night, and taping the lid into place","Topical antibiotic drops applied four times daily"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck's conservative measures, protecting the exposed surface until surgery. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with a painful red eye is found on slit lamp to have white deposits on the back surface of the cornea. What are these called?","choices":["Follicles","Keratic precipitates","Drusen","Hard exudates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — white blood cell deposits on the corneal endothelium. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with Sjögren disease has recurrent diffuse redness of both eyes with minimal discomfort and no discharge, alongside malaise and fatigue. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Oral doxycycline for seven days","Routine referral to ophthalmology","Emergent referral the same day","A course of topical antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck manages autoimmune conjunctivitis with routine referral. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a tender red swelling at the inner corner of her lower eyelid, with purulent material expressible from the lower punctum. She is afebrile, systemically well and reliable. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Immediate surgical dacryocystorhinostomy","Outpatient oral antibiotics for ten days with warm compresses","Hospital admission for intravenous antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours","Topical antibiotic drops alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild dacryocystitis in a well, reliable patient is managed as an outpatient. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 33-year-old teacher has been started on drops for acute bacterial conjunctivitis. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Expect it to worsen over the first week before improving","Expect it to take two to three weeks to settle","Take contagious precautions with careful hand hygiene, and expect progressive improvement within days","No precautions are needed, as bacterial conjunctivitis is not contagious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck pairs the precautions with the expected prompt response. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has pain, redness and swelling over the outer third of her upper eyelid, with an enlarged node in front of the ear on the same side. Workup points to an inflammatory cause. Which treatment does the lecture give, and how quickly should it work?","choices":["Intravenous antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours","Oral corticosteroids, with a response expected within 48 hours","Oral antibiotics, with a response expected within 48 hours","Cool compresses alone, with resolution over two weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck's agent and response window for inflammatory dacryoadenitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman who sleeps in her contact lenses has severe unilateral eye pain, photophobia and blurred vision. The cornea is hazy so the iris details cannot be seen clearly, and there is a ring of redness at the corneal edge that does not spare the limbus. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Episcleritis","Bacterial keratitis","Viral conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — corneal haze with ciliary flush in a lens overwearer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has an active herpes simplex epithelial dendrite. Which action should specifically NOT be taken by the primary care provider?","choices":["Referring for slit lamp examination","Staining the cornea with fluorescein","Prescribing topical glucocorticoid drops","Prescribing an oral antiviral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck puts this prohibition in capitals and leaves the decision to ophthalmology. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A public health team is treating an area where trachoma prevalence is eight per cent. Which regimen does the lecture describe?","choices":["Azithromycin one gram orally as a single dose, given to all eligible members of the evaluation unit","Doxycycline 100 milligrams twice daily for seven days, to symptomatic individuals only","Topical erythromycin ointment to symptomatic individuals only","Azithromycin one gram weekly for four weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — mass drug administration, at or above the five per cent threshold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with itchy bilateral red eyes has bumps on the inner lid surface that are red at the surface and paler at the base. What do these indicate?","choices":["Hard exudates, which point to diabetic retinopathy","Papillae, which point to a bacterial or allergic cause","Follicles, which point to a chlamydial or viral cause","Keratic precipitates, which point to anterior uveitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — papillae are red at the surface and paler at the base. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man may have a corneal abrasion after a fingernail injury. Which examination will best demonstrate it?","choices":["Inject fluorescein intravenously and photograph the retina with a blue flash","Instil phenylephrine and observe for blanching","Measure intraocular pressure","Instil fluorescein and examine under a Wood lamp with ultraviolet light"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the surface fluorescein examination detects abrasions, ulcers and foreign bodies. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman treated for bacterial conjunctivitis 24 hours ago returns with no improvement. She wears contact lenses daily. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent referral to ophthalmology","Continue the same drops and review in one week","Switch to an oral antibiotic and review in 48 hours","Add a topical corticosteroid to settle the inflammation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both lens wear and failure to improve in 24 hours are on the deck's urgent-referral list. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a red eye with normal visual acuity, normal pupils and eye movements, a clear cornea with no fluorescein uptake, no significant pain or photophobia, and reliable follow-up. Which disposition is appropriate?","choices":["Emergent referral now","Same-day ophthalmology evaluation","Urgent referral within 24 to 48 hours","Routine follow-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all five of the notes' conditions for routine care are met. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70 (speaker notes)"}]};
